---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';

const path = '/for/beauty';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'for/beauty');
const desc = description(site, 'for/beauty');

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'What is the best Shopify email app for a beauty brand?',
    answer: 'For most beauty brands on Shopify, Klaviyo is the strongest choice once the list is large enough to justify it. Its Email plan starts at $20 a month for 500 profiles, and segments can separate active subscribers, cancelled subscribers and one-time buyers. Smaller stores can start on Omnisend, which has a free plan for up to 250 contacts and 500 emails a month.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How should beauty brands time replenishment emails?',
    answer: 'Base the timing on how long each product actually lasts. A 30 ml serum used twice a day runs out far sooner than a body lotion or a hair mask. Set a reminder window for each product, based on size and typical use, and suppress the reminder when the customer has an active subscription or has reordered recently. Fixed 30-day intervals feel random to customers and are a common cause of unsubscribes.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Should education emails come before promotions?',
    answer: 'For new customers, yes. A new buyer usually needs to know how to apply a product, in what order to use a routine and what results are realistic. Send education first, and only introduce cross-sells after the customer has had time to use what they bought. Keep track of unsubscribe rates for education and promotional sends separately so you can see whether the promotions are costing you subscribers.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How much does Klaviyo cost for a beauty store?',
    answer: 'Klaviyo prices its Email plans by active profiles. The pricing page lists $20 a month for 500 profiles, $30 for 1,000, $60 for 2,500, $100 for 5,000 and $400 for 25,000. A free plan covers 250 active profiles and 500 email sends a month. Email plus SMS plans are listed without a published figure, so SMS pricing is on request.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is Omnisend enough for a growing beauty brand?',
    answer: 'It can be, for welcome, cart, post-purchase and simple replenishment flows, particularly while the list is small. The Pro plan lists 2,500 contacts at $41.30 a month with unlimited emails. When subscription branching, shade-level segments and routine education become daily work, many beauty brands move to Klaviyo or to a platform with more detailed logic.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does Shopify Email work for replenishment campaigns?',
    answer: 'Shopify Email is built into the Shopify admin and includes 10,000 emails each month free. After that, the paid sending rate is $1 per 1,000 emails. It suits brands that need straightforward campaigns and launch announcements. Brands running several overlapping replenishment and education paths usually need a platform with stronger automation controls.',
  },
  {
    question: 'When does Drip fit a beauty store?',
    answer: 'Drip fits operators who want to build explicit workflows with tags and branches, for example separate routine paths for cleanser buyers and serum buyers. Its 1 to 2,500 people plan is listed at $39 a month with unlimited email sends and up to 50 workflows. It has a 14-day trial but no ongoing free plan.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Should beauty brands pair email with SMS for replenishment?',
    answer: 'SMS works for a single replenishment reminder when customers have opted in and the frequency is capped. Email carries the education, ingredient detail and bundle offers. Whichever app you choose, suppress SMS reminders for subscribers who were charged for the current month, so a text does not arrive the same day as a renewal.',
  },
  {
    question: 'What should beauty email programs measure?',
    answer: 'Track replenishment conversion by product, subscription retention at 90 days, bundle attach rate from email, and unsubscribe rate split between education and promotional content. Revenue per recipient is useful, but beauty margin depends on repeat purchases and subscription continuity more than on launch-week spikes.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="relative overflow-hidden py-16 md:py-24">
    <div class="container">
      <a href="/for" class="text-sm font-bold text-[#9a5b16] hover:text-[#24170f]">← All store types</a>
      <p class="editorial-kicker mt-6">Beauty & skincare</p>
      <h1 class="hero-title mt-5 max-w-5xl">Best Shopify email apps for beauty brands</h1>
      <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">
        Our pick for most beauty brands on Shopify is Klaviyo. It separates active subscribers, cancelled subscribers and one-time buyers, which is the split you need before you can time a replenishment reminder correctly, and its Email plan starts at $20 a month for 500 profiles. Smaller stores with a short list should start with Omnisend, which has a free plan for up to 250 contacts.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-3xl text-lg leading-8 text-[#5d3a1d]">
        This page compares the seven apps beauty merchants ask about most: Klaviyo, Omnisend, Shopify Email, Mailchimp, Privy, Drip and Seguno. Each price is the figure on the vendor's public pricing page as of 7 October 2026, with the plan and billing basis stated next to it.
      </p>
      <div class="mt-9 flex flex-wrap gap-3">
        <a class="btn btn-primary" href="/apps">Full app rankings</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/use-cases/replenishment">Replenishment guide</a>
        <a class="btn btn-secondary" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-omnisend">Klaviyo vs Omnisend</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container pb-12">
    <TldrBox
      title="Top Shopify email apps for beauty brands"
      picks={[
        { label: 'Best overall', tool: 'Klaviyo', reason: 'Segments by subscription state and purchase history, with a Email plan from $20 a month for 500 profiles. Suited to beauty catalogues with many shades and bundles.' },
        { label: 'Best for a small list', tool: 'Omnisend', reason: 'Free plan for up to 250 contacts and 500 emails a month, with email, SMS and push in one builder.' },
        { label: 'Best if you want no extra app cost', tool: 'Shopify Email', reason: 'Built into the Shopify admin, with 10,000 emails a month free and $1 per 1,000 emails after that.' },
        { label: 'Best for an existing Mailchimp account', tool: 'Mailchimp', reason: 'Free plan for up to 250 contacts; paid Standard plan from $20 a month at the 500-contact tier.' },
        { label: 'Best for pay-by-contact simplicity', tool: 'Privy', reason: 'Email from $30 a month, where you pay only for mailable contacts and send unlimited emails.' },
        { label: 'Best workflow builder', tool: 'Drip', reason: 'Tag-based workflows for separate routine paths, from $39 a month for up to 2,500 people with unlimited sends.' },
      ]}
    />

    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">What a beauty email program has to do</h2>
      <p>
        Beauty email has three jobs that other retail verticals only partly share. It has to time replenishment around how long each product lasts, it has to teach a routine before it sells the next step, and it has to stay quiet when a subscription renewal is about to be charged. Most email apps can send all three kinds of message. The difference lies in whether they can read the subscription and order state well enough to hold back the wrong message at the right moment.
      </p>
      <p>
        Subscription apps on Shopify handle billing, so the email tool has to read that state from the store and respect it. A replenishment promotion that lands on the day a box is charged damages trust quickly. When you evaluate an app, ask how it receives order and subscription status, and whether you can build a suppression rule for "charged this month" without writing custom code.
      </p>
      <p>
        Claims are the other constraint. Ingredient and results language is regulated in many markets, and no email app reviews your copy for you. Whichever tool you pick, build a sign-off step into the template process so that urgency rewrites go through the same review as the original.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="ledger-card mb-12 overflow-x-auto">
      <p class="editorial-kicker">Comparison table</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Seven Shopify email apps for beauty brands</h2>
      <table class="mt-8 w-full min-w-[720px] text-left text-sm">
        <thead>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/15 text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-wider text-[#9a5b16]">
            <th class="py-3 pr-4">App</th>
            <th class="py-3 pr-4">Best for beauty</th>
            <th class="py-3 pr-4">Starting price (plan, billing)</th>
            <th class="py-3">Key limitation</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody class="text-[#4a2d17]">
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/8">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]">1. Klaviyo</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Large catalogues with shade and subscription segments</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Email plan $20/month for 500 profiles (monthly); free plan for 250 profiles</td>
            <td class="py-3">Cost rises with every active profile, so list hygiene matters</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/8">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]">2. Omnisend</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Small and growing DTC beauty lists needing email and SMS</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Free for 250 contacts and 500 emails/month; Standard $11.20/month for 500 contacts (monthly, introductory 30% discount shown for three months)</td>
            <td class="py-3">Branching for complex subscription logic is lighter than Klaviyo's</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/8">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]">3. Shopify Email</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Stores that want campaigns without a separate vendor</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">First 10,000 emails a month free, then $1 per 1,000 emails (per-email)</td>
            <td class="py-3">Simpler automation than a dedicated platform for overlapping replenishment paths</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/8">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]">4. Mailchimp</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Brands already running Mailchimp audiences</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Free for up to 250 contacts and 500 sends/month; Standard from $20/month at the 500-contact tier (monthly)</td>
            <td class="py-3">Send allowance and price step up by contact tier</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/8">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]">5. Privy</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Stores growing a list from pop-ups and sign-up forms</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Privy Email from $30/month (monthly); billed on mailable contacts; no ongoing free plan</td>
            <td class="py-3">Pricing depends on mailable contacts, so an unsegmented list costs more</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/8">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]">6. Drip</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Operators who build explicit tag-based routine paths</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">$39/month for 1–2,500 people with unlimited sends (monthly); 14-day trial, no ongoing free plan</td>
            <td class="py-3">Requires more hands-on workflow building than template-led tools</td>
          </tr>
          <tr class="border-b border-[#24170f]/8">
            <td class="py-3 pr-4 font-bold text-[#24170f]">7. Seguno</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Shopify stores that want to compare a further option</td>
            <td class="py-3 pr-4">Pricing on request</td>
            <td class="py-3">No published price to compare against the others</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container space-y-12 pb-16">
    <div class="prose-shopify">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Klaviyo: best overall for a beauty catalogue</h2>
      <p>
        Klaviyo is the strongest fit when your beauty range has many shades, bundles and subscription states. It segments by purchase history and subscription status, so you can send a routine-education flow to a new serum buyer and a skip-recovery flow to a subscriber who has paused, without the two lists overlapping. The Email plan lists $20 a month for 500 profiles, $60 for 2,500 and $400 for 25,000, all billed monthly on the pricing page. A free plan covers 250 active profiles and 500 email sends a month.
      </p>
      <p>
        The cost is the main limitation. Because the plan is priced per profile, every broad sign-up source, such as a giveaway or a sampling programme, raises the bill whether or not those people buy. At 2,500 profiles the Email plan comes to $60 a month, or $720 a year. Sunset inactive profiles and keep the active list small, or the price will drift upward. SMS plans are listed without a published figure, so you will need to ask for SMS pricing directly.
      </p>
      <p>
        Choose Klaviyo when you already have a steady order volume, someone who owns retention, and a need for detailed segments across shades and bundles. For the replenishment setup, see the <a class="link" href="/use-cases/replenishment">replenishment guide</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Omnisend: best for a small list that also needs SMS</h2>
      <p>
        Omnisend suits beauty brands that are still small and want welcome, cart, post-purchase and replenishment reminders running quickly. It keeps email, SMS and web push in a single builder, so you do not manage three tools for one customer. The free plan covers 250 contacts with 500 emails and 500 web push notifications a month, which is enough to test a welcome series and a post-purchase education flow before you spend anything.
      </p>
      <p>
        Its limits show once the business grows. Segmentation covers most small beauty scenarios, but it is lighter than Klaviyo's when you need intricate subscription branching or shade-level logic across hundreds of variants. The Standard plan is listed at $11.20 a month for 500 contacts and 6,000 emails, with a 30% introductory discount shown for the first three months. The Pro plan is $41.30 a month for 2,500 contacts with unlimited emails, which works out to $495.60 a year.
      </p>
      <p>
        Omnisend fits a beauty store with a short list and a simple automation plan. Many brands move to Klaviyo or a more detailed platform once subscription logic becomes a daily task.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Shopify Email: best if you want no extra vendor</h2>
      <p>
        Shopify Email is built into the Shopify admin, so the setup sits inside the store you already run. The first 10,000 emails each month are free, and after that you pay $1 per 1,000 emails. For a beauty brand that sends a few launch announcements and occasional newsletters, that pricing is hard to beat.
      </p>
      <p>
        The limitation is depth. Shopify Email is a good tool for campaigns, but it is not designed for several overlapping replenishment paths, routine education sequences and suppression rules that depend on subscription state. If your replenishment program needs those, use Shopify Email for launches and newsletters and a dedicated platform for lifecycle flows, or move the whole program to one of the other apps on this page.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Mailchimp: best if your audience already lives there</h2>
      <p>
        Mailchimp has a free plan for up to 250 contacts and 500 sends a month, with a maximum of 250 sends per day. The paid Standard plan starts at $20 a month at the 500-contact tier and covers up to 6,000 emails a month at that level. If your beauty brand already has a Mailchimp audience with years of history, moving it costs time and deliverability, so staying can be the sensible choice.
      </p>
      <p>
        Price and send allowance both step up with contact tiers, so a list that grows quickly will move into more expensive bands. Check the tier you will be in after six months of growth, not only the one you start in, and compare that with the cost of a platform that charges on different terms.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Privy: best for a list built from pop-ups and sign-up forms</h2>
      <p>
        Privy is built around capturing emails from pop-ups and sign-up forms, then sending to them. Its Email plan is listed at $30 a month, and the pricing page says you pay only for mailable contacts while sending unlimited emails. That model suits beauty stores whose list grows from quiz and sample sign-ups, where a large share of contacts never buy.
      </p>
      <p>
        Privy has no ongoing free plan. Its SMS add-on includes 250 free credits, with paid credits chosen by monthly volume. Because the bill follows mailable contacts, a list that is not segmented early can grow more costly than expected. Keep a sunset rule for people who have not engaged in a set period.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Drip: best workflow builder for routine paths</h2>
      <p>
        Drip is for operators who want to see every branch of a workflow. You can tag customers by routine step, skin concern or shade, then send each tag group to a separate path. A three-step skincare introduction, for example, can route a cleanser buyer to toner education and a serum buyer to a moisturiser suggestion, and keep subscribers out of one-time replenishment promotions.
      </p>
      <p>
        The 1 to 2,500 people plan is listed at $39 a month with unlimited email sends and up to 50 workflows. It has a 14-day trial with no credit card required, but no ongoing free plan. Because the builder is hands-on, plan for someone on your team to maintain the workflows, rather than relying on templates.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="prose-shopify">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Seguno: include it if it fits your Shopify setup</h2>
      <p>
        Seguno is on this list because beauty merchants ask about it. Pricing is on request, so compare it with the published plans above by asking the vendor for a quote. Before shortlisting it, check whether it covers the replenishment, subscription-suppression and SMS requirements in the checklist below, since those are the points that differ most between apps.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container py-12">
    <div class="card">
      <p class="editorial-kicker">How to choose</p>
      <h2 class="mt-3 font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Match the app to list size and subscription logic</h2>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-8 text-[#4a2d17]">
        Start with two numbers: the number of profiles or contacts you will hold in twelve months, and the number of subscription or replenishment paths you need to run at the same time. If the list is under 250 contacts, the free plans on Omnisend, Mailchimp or Klaviyo cover the basics. If you run one or two replenishment paths, Shopify Email or Omnisend can do the job. If you run several paths that depend on subscription state, shortlist Klaviyo or Drip and test the suppression rules before you commit.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-8 text-[#4a2d17]">
        Then check the cost at your projected size, not today's. Klaviyo's Email plan at 2,500 profiles is $60 a month, while Omnisend's Pro plan at 2,500 contacts is $41.30 a month. Privy is billed on mailable contacts, and Shopify Email charges $1 per 1,000 emails after the first 10,000 each month. Calculate the figure for twelve months of growth, including sign-up imports, and compare that total across the apps you shortlist.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container py-12">
    <div class="grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="ledger-card">
        <p class="editorial-kicker">Replenishment setup</p>
        <h2 class="mt-3 font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Set timing by product, not by calendar</h2>
        <ol class="mt-4 list-decimal space-y-3 pl-5 text-[17px] leading-8 text-[#4a2d17]">
          <li>List each product with its size and typical use per day or week.</li>
          <li>Set a first reminder shortly before the product should run out, and a second one only if the first gets no reorder.</li>
          <li>Suppress the reminder for customers with an active subscription or a reorder in the last few weeks.</li>
          <li>Review reorder rates by product every month and adjust the windows from your own data.</li>
        </ol>
      </div>
      <div class="ledger-card">
        <p class="editorial-kicker">Education and promotion</p>
        <h2 class="mt-3 font-serif text-3xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Teach first, then cross-sell</h2>
        <p class="mt-4 text-[17px] leading-8 text-[#4a2d17]">
          New buyers need a short education run that explains how to use what they bought, in what order to apply it and what results are realistic. Introduce cross-sells after that run, and only for products that fit the routine. Track unsubscribe rates for education and promotional sends separately. If promotional unsubscribes run well ahead of education unsubscribes, reduce the promotional frequency before sending more.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container py-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Deliverability and claims review</h2>
      <p>
        Beauty lists are sensitive to frequency, so keep promotional and education sends on separate schedules, and send promotions to people who have opened or clicked recently. Warm up any new sending domain before a launch or a sale, and avoid sending a full list on day one of a new tool. The <a class="link" href="/use-cases/deliverability">deliverability guide</a> covers the infrastructure side in more detail.
      </p>
      <p>
        Put a claims review in front of every template that mentions results, ingredients or urgency. No app on this list performs that review for you, and a regulatory issue costs far more than a delayed send. Keep EU and US subscribers on separate lists where your consent records require it, and use copy that fits each market's rules.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container py-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify max-w-4xl">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-4xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Testing before you switch</h2>
      <p>
        Test each shortlisted app on a staff account before you move a live list. Check four things: that a new subscriber receives the right welcome branch for the source they signed up from, that cart recovery skips anyone who bought the same variant recently, that post-purchase emails hold back discounts for gift buyers, and that a winback flow stops after a fixed number of messages. If any of these fails in the first week of testing, the app will not hold up during a sale.
      </p>
      <p>
        Move the list during a quiet period, not in the run-up to a launch. Keep the old tool's automations paused until the new one is sending correctly, import only engaged contacts, and allow about three weeks for the old and new welcome and cart flows to overlap before retiring the old ones.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="container pb-8">
    <div class="flex flex-wrap gap-4 text-sm font-bold">
      <a class="link" href="/apps">All Shopify email apps →</a>
      <a class="link" href="/use-cases/replenishment">Replenishment workflows →</a>
      <a class="link" href="/for/supplements">Supplement stores →</a>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Beauty brand email FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
